  "language": "english",
  "text": "We have $500 to win $175K. Shout out to this creator and his brother. Definitely a dope position to be in. Like you said, $500 to win $175K. We've talked about this before in college football and NFL where people end up in these situations where they have these really, really, really huge long odds payoffs. And everybody talks about EV, not EV, but something pretty much everybody ignores is total percentage of bankroll. I'm really not gonna presume anything in this situation. This person bet $500 on a pretty long shot odds, so their bankroll could be very large. Maybe this bankroll could be 50, 100,000, maybe even more. And in this situation, I would actually totally agree with the analysis that you probably should just let it ride. It is an overall such a small percentage of your bankroll and the winning is obviously a fairly large payout. The thing I would caution is if that $500 is not close to 1% and it's closer to 5%, 10%, cashing out is going to be the optimal answer. Say this bankroll is only $10,000 and they can cash out for $4,000. They're going to increase their bankroll by 40%. That means that the amount that they can bet for all subsequent bets from here on out are 40% more, meaning their ROI over time is 40% more on that compounding. So I don't have all the information. I'm not saying that this person does or doesn't have a large or small bankroll. I have no idea, but this is more an educational point of this. Sure, you can go and do and look at calculators and they're going to bear some good math for you. But ultimately, one of the very, very important things you need to calculate and understand is the proportion of your bankroll that you can win by cashing out. If that is, in my opinion, greater than 15, say 20%, you should be taking the cash out in this situation because it compounds on all the rest of your bets. But I don't know, let me know in the comments what do you guys think? Should they cash out? Should they let this ride? How do you guys think about problems like this?",
